#{[17592186436610 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)={cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}).$"] [17592186436610 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186462516 :answer-field/key "$y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$"] [17592186534012 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm 4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.86$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.86$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/50/50b71bd4aefb94179b55a94992f9e7a42b9ddbd50cd3a9dcf17645f4afd4846c.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f60bdf0ec9c734a1d0d352c448b2c829869ba9fe0115bd860f639b9e4dc83cd0.png)"] [17592186534012 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.86$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.86$ | $1.48$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/50/50b71bd4aefb94179b55a94992f9e7a42b9ddbd50cd3a9dcf17645f4afd4846c.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)|\\le 1$ but ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{diverges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f60bdf0ec9c734a1d0d352c448b2c829869ba9fe0115bd860f639b9e4dc83cd0.png)"] [17592186534066 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}."] [17592186566497 :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})"] [17592186566544 :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)"] [17592186566638 :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(2y)"] [17592186566724 :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡(x-y)"] [17592186566781 :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-y)"] [17592186566828 :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)"] [17592186567042 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$"] [17592186567047 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582344 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\int \\frac{1}{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186694089 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 6 xe^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186694090 :question/problem "$\\int x3^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186694098 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x4^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186694100 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 2 xe^{- x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186694108 :question/problem "$\\int 6 xe^{7 x - 4}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186694125 :question/problem "$\\int 8 xe^{6 x}\\,\\text{d}x=$"]}
